**Convention 1.4 (credited factors and canonicalization).** Set
$C_0=\langle\alpha,\alpha\rangle$ and

$$
S_1=\{\alpha,\beta,C_0\},\qquad
S_{d+1}=\{\alpha,\beta\}\sqcup\{\langle x,y\rangle:x,y\in S_d\},
\qquad m_d=2^d-1.
\tag{OOA.3}
$$

Reuse [AAR, §§12–13][AAR]'s complete temporal factor. At an original
branch endpoint $\Gamma_q(u)=(0,0)$. Otherwise let the first blocking
leaf be $\ell$ at $a$, write $q=ar$, and put
$e(\alpha)=0,e(\beta)=1$. Then

$$
\Gamma_q(u)=
\begin{cases}
(2-e(\ell),1-e(\ell)),&r=\varepsilon,\\
(\#L(r)+2\#R(r)+2-e(\ell),\mathbf1_{r\text{ ends in }R}),&r\ne\varepsilon.
\end{cases}
\tag{OOA.4}
$$

On attained factors the credited decoder is

$$
\operatorname{Pred}((b,c),k)=
\begin{cases}
\mathsf{branch},&k\ge b,\\
\beta,&b\ge1,\ k=b-1,\\
\alpha,&c=1,\ k=b-2,\\
\mathsf{absent},&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\tag{OOA.5}
$$

It equals $r_{\rho^ku}(q)$ for every $k\ge0$. The whole trajectory
recovers $b$ as its first branch count and $c$ by its alpha stage.
Write $h(z)=\operatorname{Pred}(z,0)$ and $G_q=\Gamma_q[S_d]$.
The factor vector on $W_d$ separates $S_d$.

[FMR, Lemma 2.2 and Theorems 2.3–2.5][FMR] supply completion
$\pi_d(v)\in S_d$, fixing $S_d$ and preserving all boundary replies
and factors, including variable shapes. On a canonical block every
arbitrary finite probe is a function of one boundary reply in that same
block, or a constant. In particular, a length-$d$ reply is alpha exactly
when its length-$(d-1)$ parent is branch; longer words are absent.

